# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Built asset packs — hosted on the website, not in git
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
packs/
game/assets/atlases/

# Built content packs (tools/build_packs.py, phase 0.3). `*.pck` above already covers
# the art half; these are the zips. 2.2 MB each and rebuildable byte-for-byte from
# `scenarios/` — the builder is deterministic precisely so that holds.
web/downloads/*.zip

# ⚠️ `web/downloads/packs.json` IS COMMITTED, AND IT IS THE ONE ARTEFACT THAT MUST BE.
# It is generated, so the reflex is to ignore it — but `build_packs.py` reads it as the
# record of WHAT IS ALREADY PUBLISHED, and that is what makes the version guard work:
# content changed under an unchanged `version` is refused because the committed manifest
# still holds the old checksum. Ignore it and a fresh clone has no baseline, the guard
# never fires, and every existing install goes silently stale.

# Staged 0 A.D. audio (tools/stage_audio.py). Same call as the atlases above:
# it IS redistributable -- 0 A.D. is CC-BY-SA 3.0, unlike the itch.io UI packs
# -- but it is ~52 MB of derived files that reach players through the audio
# pack (PLAN.md 3.2, pack_audio_v1.pck), not through git. Re-stage with
#     python tools/stage_audio.py
game/assets/audio/

# GONE 2026-08-30, and this is the line the whole UI overhaul was for.
#
# `game/assets/ui/{control_groups,menu,hud}/` were Kibyra copies: usable in a
# built game, not redistributable, therefore gitignored -- which meant a clean
# clone of this repo produced a game with NO PANELS, and every developer had to
# download two itch.io packs by hand before they could see the HUD. Those three
# directories no longer exist. `game/assets/ui/` is committed in full, a fresh
# clone runs with its chrome intact, and `tools/licence_audit.py` stopped having
# 14 undeclared files in it.
#
# Nothing replaces these lines. They are recorded rather than deleted because
# "the UI art is gitignored" was true for the whole life of the project until
# today and is written into a good many comments that have not caught up.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Python tooling
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
tools_env/

# Per-machine isobake paths (Blender, 0 A.D. checkout, output dir).
# PLAN.md §2.6: raw-art paths are never committed. The committed camera config
# is tools/isobake.toml; see tools/isobake.local.toml.example.
tools/isobake.local.toml

# Optional, editor-only override for where campaigns are read from (PLAN.md 3.3,
# `Campaigns.LOCAL_CONFIG`). NOT REQUIRED, unlike isobake's: `scenarios/` is the
# sibling of the Godot project on every clone, so the loader derives the path and a
# fresh checkout runs the campaign with no setup step. This file exists only for a
# layout that differs.
game/content.local.json

# The same arrangement for the MapMaker (PLAN.md 16.1, `GameRoot.LOCAL_CONFIG`), and
# NOT REQUIRED for the same reason: `game/` is the sibling of `MapMaker/` on every
# clone, so the tool derives the path. This file exists only for a layout that differs.
MapMaker/mapmaker.local.json

# The MapMaker is a SECOND Godot project, so it has its own import cache. `.godot/`
# is already ignored at the top of this file by directory name, but the pattern is
# anchored, so the second project needs saying out loud.
MapMaker/.godot/

.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Secrets
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .env holds the Vikunja API token (kanban/README.md). It was NOT ignored until
# 2026-09-01 and `origin` is a public GitHub repo, so it was one `git add -A`
# away from a published credential. A Vikunja token grants read+write on every
# project the issuing user can see; there is no scoping down to one board.
.env

.pytest_cache/

