AgeOfDragons.exe

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Kanban tools — LOCAL, never cloned (owner's ruling 2026-09-01)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `card.py`, `card_game.py` and their README talk to a private Vikunja instance with
# a token from `.env`. They are how the two agents track work; they are no part of
# the game and mean nothing to a contributor who clones this repo.
#
# THIS RULE WAS ALREADY HERE AND THE FOLDER SHIPPED ANYWAY, which is the thing worth
# remembering: **.gitignore does not untrack what git is already tracking.** All three
# files were committed before the rule was added (`ff141cd`, `917ef8b`, `9e0d743`), so
# git went on tracking them and the ignore line was silently doing nothing. Untracked
# with `git rm -r --cached kanban` on 2026-09-01; the files stay on disk.
#
# They remain in HISTORY. Removing them from the tip is what stops a clone getting
# them; purging them from every commit needs a rewrite nobody has asked for. Nothing
# secret is in them -- `.env` was never committed (see Secrets above, verified with
# `git log --all -- .env`) -- so the history is an untidiness, not an exposure.
kanban/
MapMaker.exe
